From: gts7l project
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: qcom: optionally drop the hot-plug slot for soldered
 endpoints

The qcom Root Ports advertise a hot-plug capable slot (SLTCAP HPC, ABP,
PCP, MRLSP, ...). pci_bridge_d3_possible() refuses D3 for a hot-plug
bridge that pciehp doesn't drive (and for pciehp-driven ones on non-x86,
unless forced), so the Root Port stays in D0 across system suspend.
pci_host_common_d3cold_possible() then fails and dw_pcie_suspend_noirq()
returns without sending PME_Turn_Off: the link never goes to L2 and the
controller is never deinitialised, so its clocks (pcie_0_aux, PHY aux and
refgen) and with them the CXO vote stay on in s2idle.

On boards whose endpoint is soldered down there is no slot. Add an
optional "qcom,no-hotplug-slot" controller property that clears the slot
capabilities, letting the Root Port reach D3 and the link L2 in suspend.

diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
index d8eb52857..280dff31d 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
@@ -372,6 +372,15 @@ static void qcom_pcie_set_slot_nccs(struct dw_pcie *pci)
 	 */
 	val = readl(pci->dbi_base + offset + PCI_EXP_SLTCAP);
 	val |= PCI_EXP_SLTCAP_NCCS;
+	/*
+	 * A soldered-down endpoint (e.g. a Wi-Fi chip) sits in no real slot.
+	 * Advertising hot-plug makes the PCI core refuse D3 for the Root Port
+	 * (pci_bridge_d3_possible()), which in turn stops system suspend from
+	 * taking the link to L2 and turning the controller off
+	 * (dw_pcie_suspend_noirq()), leaving its clocks and CXO on.
+	 */
+	if (of_property_read_bool(pci->dev->of_node, "qcom,no-hotplug-slot"))
+		val &= ~(PCIE_CAP_SLOT_VAL | PCI_EXP_SLTCAP_HPC);
 	writel(val, pci->dbi_base + offset + PCI_EXP_SLTCAP);
 
 	dw_pcie_dbi_ro_wr_dis(pci);
